From 0870cb832a329bc85e380237428b9fa6cf103827 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 15:13:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] FEX: watch each swapchain copy's fence from its submit; 30 ms
 hold-back ceiling; cap grid from every held present

Three fixes from the b61 log with VKD3D fex7 (Jesse, 2026-10-24):

1. Frame done thread got each copy only once vkQueuePresentKHR returned,
   i.e. after the cap's release. A GPU finish before that release was
   noted at the release, so frame start pacing saw ~0 spare time for
   every frame whose present didn't wait for the queue thread. 19 seconds
   of the run had a 'spare min' of about 0 (fex6: 3), each one pulled the
   hold-back down for 64 frames; medium scenes reached the screen 54.6 ms
   after the frame start (fex6: 50.9), calm scenes ~62 ms. Now submit_blit
   adds the copy (fence + blit value) to the thread's ring right after its
   submit, the thread waits for its fence at once and notes the GPU finish
   as it happens; the present itself is handed over after
   vkQueuePresentKHR as before and only then signalled on the frame done
   timeline. A retried present's earlier copies signal nothing (fex7 would
   have reused a fence still in flight there). Present line gets
   'GPU ahead' (GPU done this long before vkQueuePresentKHR returned).

2. Hold-back ceiling 20 -> 30 ms. In the b61 run with fex4 the hold-back
   sat at 20 ms for 78 of 253 s with a median of 6 ms more measured spare
   time above it. Frame start line gets 'spare times allow X ms (ceiling
   30 ms, more than that N)'.

3. Cap pre-sleep measures the cap's grid from every present the cap held
   3 ms or more, pre-slept or not (the pre-sleep wakes 4 ms early, so such
   a present came out at the cap's own due time). The checks every 150
   presents that went without pre-sleep held the VkQueue ~33 ms in
   vkQueuePresentKHR; copy/compute submits waited up to 33 ms behind them
   and seconds with a check had twice as many frames of 40 ms or more
   (13 of 51 vs 24 of 188).

